; A proof the checker accepts but the semantics does not model.
;
; `U` is a sort *constructor* -- a sort of arity 1 -- and the SMT-LIB
; formalization has no counterpart for one applied to a sort. So the terms here
; have no translation, the first side condition of the soundness theorem fails,
; and the theorem says nothing about this proof.
;
; Expected verdict: incomplete (see unmodeled.expected).
;
; This is the verdict most worth having a test for. The derivation itself is
; fine -- the same shape as hello.proof, which comes back `correct` -- so the
; only thing separating them is whether the specification covers the terms. A
; checker that collapsed `incomplete` into `correct` would claim the soundness
; theorem applied here when it does not, and nothing else in the suite would
; notice.

(declare-sort U 1)
(declare-const a (U Int))
(declare-const b (U Int))
(assume @p0 (= a b))
(assume @p1 (not (= b a)))
(step @p2 :rule symm :premises (@p0))
(step @p3 :rule contra :premises (@p2 @p1))
